            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Rest your baby on your thigh in a sitting position. As you do this, be sure to support your baby's head and neck.<ref>https://kidshealth.org/en/parents/burping.html</ref> If you so choose, place a burp cloth over your and your baby's thighs to catch any spit.[[Image:Burp Babies Step 5 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Pat or rub the baby until a burp comes out. Again, this may take several minutes; it may be instantaneous. It all depends on the child. Not every feeding needs to elicit a burp, but if your baby is writhing or uncomfortable, they may need it in order to continue feeding.<ref>https://www.askdrsears.com/topics/feeding-eating/feeding-infants-toddlers/burping-baby/</ref>[[Image:Burp Babies Step 9 Version 2.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Rest your baby on your thigh in a sitting position. As you do this, be sure to support your baby's head and neck.<ref>http://kidshealth.org/parent/pregnancy_center/newborn_care/burping.html</ref> If you so choose, place a burp cloth over your and your baby's thighs to catch any spit.[[Image:Burp Babies Step 5 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Pat or rub the baby until a burp comes out. Again, this may take several minutes; it may be instantaneous. It all depends on the child. Not every feeding needs to elicit a burp, but if your baby is writhing or uncomfortable, they may need it in order to continue feeding.<ref>http://www.askdrsears.com/topics/feeding-infants-toddlers/burping-baby</ref>[[Image:Burp Babies Step 9 Version 2.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
