                "added": [
                    "==Quick Overview==",
                    "To burp your baby, hold them over your shoulder and put a burping cloth on your shoulder. Then, you want to pat them gently between the shoulder blades or rub their back using a circular motion. Always listen out for the baby burping, and when you hear them doing so, stop patting their back and put them in front of you. Now, show them your pleased by smiling at your baby and give them a kiss to let them know you’re there. Finally, you can try frequent but light breastfeeding sessions instead of bottle feeding to reduce the need to burp your baby."
                ],
                "removed": []
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "23851161",
            "user": "Aiosis",
            "timestamp": "16:42, 12 January 2018",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(7,472 bytes)",
            "change": 7396,
            "summary": "(Reverted edits by 157.246.120.55 (Talk); changed back to last version by Bananagal)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "revert",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "Burping helps babies release air that becomes trapped in their stomach. Burping is often most effective right after or in between a feeding, when a baby sucks in extra air while feeding on milk or food. Burping your baby will help him or her release that air, improving their feeding and general comfort. Burping is quite easy if you know what to do.",
                    "",
                    "[[Category:Baby Health]]",
                    "== Steps ==",
                    "=== Burping Over the Shoulder===",
                    "# Hold the baby over your shoulder. Be sure to support your child's head and neck while you do this. You might have more success lifting your baby higher, so that his or her belly is resting on your shoulder.[[Image:Burp Babies Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*You might want to put a burping cloth over your shoulder, especially if the child is under a year old. The bottom of their esophagus (the tube that brings food into the stomach) is not all the way developed and may be briefly sent out of whack when the baby burps. This sometimes causes them to spit up, but is perfectly natural.",
                    "# Pat them between the shoulder blades. ''Do this very gently.'' You should only pat them using wrist action; do not move your arm in order to burp your baby.[[Image:Burp Babies Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*If you don't want to pat your baby, you can also rub his or her back using a circular motion with your hand. Although a bit less effective, it often accomplishes the same thing.",
                    "# Know when the baby burped, and stop patting. It might sound like a normal burp, and if it does, you know to stop. Otherwise, it could sound like a sneeze, a grunt, or just a brief, \"Ump.\"[[Image:Burp Babies Step 3 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#  Put them in front of you as soon as they burp, and show them your smiling face. Establish your presence again and give your baby a kiss.[[Image:Burp Babies Step 4 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "=== Burping Sitting up===",
                    "#Rest your baby on your thigh in a sitting position. As you do this, be sure to support your baby's head and neck.<ref>http://kidshealth.org/parent/pregnancy_center/newborn_care/burping.html</ref> If you so choose, place a burp cloth over your and your baby's thighs to catch any spit.[[Image:Burp Babies Step 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Support your baby's front chest with your hands and their neck and head with your fingers. This will make sure that your baby is in a secure position and their upper extremities are supported at all times.",
                    "#Pat, rub, or lightly bounce your baby until he or she burps. There are several ways you can try to elicit a burp from your baby, although it might take several minutes until they need to. They include:[[Image:Burp Babies Step 6.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Patting. Pat very lightly, using mostly wrist action, not the full force of your arm.",
                    "#*Rubbing. Rub using circular motions.",
                    "#*Bouncing. Lightly bounce your baby making sure that his or her neck is fully supported.",
                    "#Finish feeding your baby after they burp. Your baby may struggle to burp once, or may burp several times in the course of a feeding. It all depends on their individual constitutions.[[Image:Burp Babies Step 7.jpg|center]]",
                    "=== Burping Lying Down===",
                    "#Lay the baby down with their belly over your thigh and their neck and head above their body. Remember to support their neck and head with a hand on their chest in order to keep it stabilized.[[Image:Burp Babies Step 8.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Pat or rub the baby until a burp comes out. Again, this may take several minutes; it may be instantaneous. It all depends on the child. Not every feeding needs to elicit a burp, but if your baby is writhing or uncomfortable, they may need it in order to continue feeding.<ref>http://www.askdrsears.com/topics/feeding-infants-toddlers/burping-baby</ref>[[Image:Burp Babies Step 9.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Finish feeding your baby after they burp. Your baby may struggle to burp once, or may burp several times in the course of a feeding. It all depends on their individual constitutions.[[Image:Burp Babies Step 10.jpg|center]]",
                    "===Making the Burping Process Easier===",
                    "#Try to breastfeed, not bottle-feed. Breastfeeding is an easier way to avoid the need to burp, as the flow of milk is much more restricted. Bottle-feeding often forces the babies to gulp air along with the milk.[[Image:Burp Babies Step 11.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Feed the baby in a (more or less) upright position. Hold your baby at a 45° angle when breast- or bottle-feeding. This lowers the chance that the baby will need to burp by positioning the baby in an easier position to swallow.[[Image:Burp Babies Step 12.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Aim for frequent, lighter feedings. Long, heavy feedings present a higher chance of letting the baby gulp in excess air. Try to keep the feedings light and frequent instead.[[Image:Burp Babies Step 13.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Get feedback from your baby about whether they need to burp. In between suckling, look at your baby and try to gauge their comfort level. An uncomfortable grimace on your baby's otherwise pleasant lips may mean that she needs to burp. If the baby relaxes her face again and seems content, the urge to burp has probably passed.[[Image:Burp Babies Step 14.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Know that not every feeding is accompanied by burping. Some babies burp more than others, and even then, there will come times when your otherwise burp-happy baby won't need to burp. As your baby grows older, their ability to self-regulate will improve and their need to burp will fade.[[Image:Burp Babies Step 15.jpg|center]]",
                    "",
                    "== Video ==",
                    "{{Video:Burp Babies|}}",
                    "",
                    "== Tips ==",
                    "*Sometimes a crying baby is irritated by air building up in their stomach and just needs to burp. Try burping them if they are crying, you've changed them, fed them and nothing's working.",
                    "*Pat gently.",
                    "*Sometimes it helps to rub, not pat, but if you do this, keep your hand very lightly on Baby's back.",
                    "*Know the difference between spitting up and vomiting. Spitting up means that the expelled mixture is thick, comes in a small quantity, and the baby is not overly-upset/distressed. Vomiting means the baby is throwing up huge amounts, is crying/in severe distress, and the substance coming out is more of a liquid than a solid. This is serious in an infant, since babies dehydrate easily. Call your doctor, and don't be alarmed if the pediatrician tells you to bring the baby to the nearest emergency department. Depending on how long the vomiting episode has lasted and how sick the baby otherwise seems, the baby may need antibiotics, ICU monitoring and/or a saline solution delivered by intravenous catheter to prevent/stop dehydration (a serious issue in infants).",
                    "*Use a spit-up rag, receiving blanket, bath towel, or something else to keep your clothes safe in case of a spitting-up.",
                    "",
                    "== Warnings ==",
                    "*DON'T SLING THE BABY OVER YOUR SHOULDER! Hold most of him/her over your chest. If you put them too far over, the baby could suffocate between your back and the chair you sit in, or fall over your shoulder and onto the floor. If that were to happen, you would not, no way, no how, be able to turn around and catch them in time!",
                    "*PAT GENTLY! If you pat too hard, you may cause permanent injuries that may result in loss of mobility, partial or complete, impairment in development, or even death.",
                    "",
                    "== Things You'll Need ==",
                    "*Baby that recently ate/drank",
                    "*Receiving blanket, spit-up rag, or bath towel.",
                    "*A chair to sit down on.",
                    "",
                    "== Related wikiHows ==",
                    "*[[Make a Burp Cloth]]",
                    "*[[Swaddle a Baby]]",
                    "*[[Feed a Baby Infant Formula]]",
                    "*[[Soothe a Baby]]",
                    "",
                    "== Sources and Citations ==",
                    "{{reflist}}",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "__METHODS__"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "just beat the damn thing until it can burb it's self fuckin pussy ass babies"
                ]
            }
        },
