            "id": "21872830",
            "user": "Votebot",
            "timestamp": "09:50, 16 February 2017",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(7,472 bytes)",
            "change": 0,
            "summary": "(reordering tips and warnings based on votes)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "adjustment",
            "reverted_by": "Joel.Miles925",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "*Know the difference between spitting up and vomiting. Spitting up means that the expelled mixture is thick, comes in a small quantity, and the baby is not overly-upset/distressed. Vomiting means the baby is throwing up huge amounts, is crying/in severe distress, and the substance coming out is more of a liquid than a solid. This is serious in an infant, since babies dehydrate easily. Call your doctor, and don't be alarmed if the pediatrician tells you to bring the baby to the nearest emergency department. Depending on how long the vomiting episode has lasted and how sick the baby otherwise seems, the baby may need antibiotics, ICU monitoring and/or a saline solution delivered by intravenous catheter to prevent/stop dehydration (a serious issue in infants)."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "*Know the difference between spitting up and vomiting. Spitting up means that the expelled mixture is thick, comes in a small quantity, and the baby is not overly-upset/distressed. Vomiting means the baby is throwing up huge amounts, is crying/in severe distress, and the substance coming out is more of a liquid than a solid. This is serious in an infant, since babies dehydrate easily. Call your doctor, and don't be alarmed if the pediatrician tells you to bring the baby to the nearest emergency department. Depending on how long the vomiting episode has lasted and how sick the baby otherwise seems, the baby may need antibiotics, ICU monitoring and/or a saline solution delivered by intravenous catheter to prevent/stop dehydration (a serious issue in infants)."
                ]
            }
        },
